If $a equiv.not 0 mod p$, then $a$ is a unit modulo $p$, so $a x equiv a y mod p$ iff $x equiv y mod p$, by cancelling $a$. Hence the numbers $a, 2a, 3a, ..., (p-1)a$ are pairwise incongruent and not congruent to $0$ modulo $p$; they must therefore be congruent to $1, 2, 3, ..., (p-1)$ in some order. Multiplying,
$ a dot 2a dot 3a dot ... dot (p-1) a equiv (p-1)! mod p, $
that is,
$ a^(p-1) (p-1)! equiv (p-1)! mod p. $
Since $(p-1)!$ is a product of units, it is itself a unit modulo $p$, so it can be cancelled to give $a^(p-1) equiv 1 mod p$. Multiplying by $a$ yields the equivalent form $a^p equiv a mod p$.
